I'd add the session's account to each `WHERE` clause and return 404 when nothing matches. `db.write()` returns `rows[0]`, so a scoped update that matches no row gives back `undefined`.

**`routes/accounts.js`**: users can only touch their own account, so the `:id` in the URL also has to equal `req.user.accountId`:

```js
router.patch("/:id", async (req, res) => {
  const row = await db.write(
    "UPDATE accounts SET display_name = $1, billing_email = $2 WHERE id = $3 AND id = $4 RETURNING *",
    [req.body.display_name, req.body.billing_email, req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.json(row);
});

router.delete("/:id", async (req, res) => {
  const row = await db.write("UPDATE accounts SET deleted_at = now() WHERE id = $1 AND id = $2 RETURNING id",
    [req.params.id, req.user.accountId]);
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.status(204).end();
});
```

**`routes/orders.js`**: this matches how the `POST` route already sets `account_id`:

```js
router.patch("/:id/cancel", async (req, res) => {
  const row = await db.write(
    "UPDATE orders SET status = 'cancelled' WHERE id = $1 AND account_id = $2 RETURNING *",
    [req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.json(row);
});
```

Use 404 rather than 403 so that probing IDs doesn't reveal which rows exist in other accounts.

The main decision is whether to also add Postgres row-level security as a backstop. `write()` is the only place a transaction starts, so it could run `SET LOCAL app.account_id = ...` before the statement, and policies would reject unscoped writes even if a future route forgets the `WHERE`. That costs a signature change to `write()`, and `query()` would need the same treatment to cover reads. With only three write routes, I'd do the `WHERE` clauses now and consider RLS once there are more.

`req.user` is also not populated as the code stands. `app.js` never installs `cookie-parser`, so `req.cookies` is undefined, `sessionUser` returns 401 on every request, and nothing the scoping depends on gets set.

Check `deleted_at` once, in the session lookup in `auth.js`, so that users of a deleted account get a 401:

```js
const rows = await db.query(
  "SELECT u.id, u.email, u.account_id FROM sessions s JOIN users u ON u.id = s.user_id JOIN accounts a ON a.id = u.account_id WHERE s.token = $1 AND s.expires_at > now() AND a.deleted_at IS NULL",
  [req.cookies && req.cookies.session]
);
```

With the scoping fix in place, every route acts only on `req.user.accountId`, so this one check covers both the accounts and orders routes. Existing sessions stop working on their next request, so the `DELETE` handler doesn't need to clean them up.

The open question is whether deletion is permanent or a grace period that ends in a hard purge. That decides whether you need a restore path or a purge job, and the code doesn't settle it. Which do you intend?

I'd add one middleware in `app.js`, registered before `sessionUser` so that rejected (401) requests get logged too. It writes one JSON line when each response finishes. By then `sessionUser` has run, so `req.user` is available for requests that passed auth.

```js
// Logs one line per request once the response is sent. Registered before
// sessionUser so rejected requests are logged too.
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const start = process.hrtime.bigint();
  res.on("finish", () => {
    console.log(JSON.stringify({
      method: req.method,
      path: req.originalUrl,
      status: res.statusCode,
      ms: Number(process.hrtime.bigint() - start) / 1e6,
      accountId: req.user && req.user.accountId,
    }));
  });
  next();
});
```

Leave out request bodies and cookies: they carry `billing_email` and the session token.

This won't log requests that die with the process during a dropped connection (the crash from the last answer), because `finish` never fires for them.

The open decision is where these logs go. Nothing in the project picks a logger yet. If you already run a log stack that wants a particular format, a library like `pino-http` would fit it better than `console.log`. Which stack is it?

