No. Raising the pool size would only delay the problem, because the pool is leaking connections.

In `db.js`, `withClient` calls `client.release()` only when `fn` succeeds:

```js
  const client = await pool.connect();
  const result = await fn(client);
  client.release();
```

If `fn` throws, for example on a failed query, the client is never returned to the pool. Each error permanently uses up one of the 20 slots.

`pool.log` fits this. Idle stays at 0 and the waiting count climbs steadily from 7 to 28, without levelling off. If 20 connections were simply too few for the traffic, the queue would rise and fall with load. Here it only grows, until requests start failing with `timeout exceeded when trying to connect`. A pool of 40 or 100 would fill up the same way, just later, and would also hold more connections open on the Postgres server.

The fix is to release the client in a `finally` block.

